<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Editorial Team</h1>
  <p>braininjurycalc.com is written and reviewed by a small editorial team at The Click Lab. Traumatic brain injury cases are among the most complex catastrophic injury matters in U.S. tort law &mdash; combining nuanced neurological analysis, neuropsychological evaluation, life-care planning, vocational impact assessment, and (in many cases) multi-defendant litigation. Getting the content right means working from primary sources: the Glasgow Coma Scale, the Rancho Los Amigos Cognitive Scale, the Disability Rating Scale, AANS/CNS clinical guidelines, peer-reviewed TBI outcome research, leading appellate decisions on catastrophic injury damages, and the published verdict and settlement databases that track TBI outcomes.</p>

  <h2>How we work</h2>
  <p>Every guide on this site follows the same workflow. A contributor drafts the article working from primary sources &mdash; severity classification systems, peer-reviewed medical literature on TBI prognosis, published life-care planning textbooks (King's, Deutsch &amp; Sawyer), state-specific catastrophic injury statutes and case law, and verdict tracker data. The editor-in-chief reviews the draft for accuracy of citation, internal consistency of damages analysis, and whether the article actually answers a question a real reader (patient, family member, or attorney) would ask. The calculator team verifies any formulas or worked examples against published settlement distributions. The final piece is published with the reviewer's byline.</p>
  <p>We do not publish "thin" content. Every guide is intended to answer a specific question a non-lawyer reader might ask &mdash; what is the typical settlement range for a moderate TBI, how is future medical care calculated, what is the difference between focal and diffuse injury, what factors affect compensation when imaging is "normal" &mdash; with enough specificity to be useful, while being honest about the limits of general informational content.</p>

  <h2>Our editorial team</h2>
  <p>Our editorial team draws on catastrophic injury and neurotrauma litigation experience spanning over a decade at firms specializing in this practice area. Team members have supported attorneys on hundreds of brain injury cases, with hands-on exposure to neuroimaging interpretation (working alongside neuroradiology experts), neuropsychological report analysis, life-care plan review, vocational rehabilitation expert coordination, and the specific procedural complexity of multi-defendant TBI litigation, including moderate-to-severe TBI litigation, life-care plan review for cognitive-impairment cases, vocational impact analysis, multi-defendant trucking cases producing TBI, and the procedural complexity of guardianship and conservatorship when a TBI patient lacks capacity to direct their own case. The team also includes medical-records and neuro-rehabilitation documentation reviewers who focus on the medical-evidence and life-care planning side of the content: the acute imaging (CT, MRI, DTI where available), the serial GCS scores, the neuropsychological battery, the rehabilitation progress notes, the Rancho-level documentation, and the life-care plan that projects future medical and attendant care needs, including neuropsychological evaluation interpretation, life-care plan construction for cognitive-impairment cases, neuro-rehabilitation documentation standards, anoxic and hypoxic-ischemic injury patterns, and the specific evidentiary challenges of mild TBI cases where imaging is "negative." This day-to-day work reviewing neurotrauma medical records, life-care plans, and neuropsychological reports for accuracy and litigation use is what qualifies the team for editorial review of TBI content: the same skills that spot a misclassified GCS or a life-care plan with unrealistic attendant care assumptions translate directly into editorial accuracy. The team wrote the compensation methodology content on this site and reviews every piece for technical accuracy against the underlying medical and legal sources, citing AANS/CNS guidelines, the Brain Injury Association of America (BIAA) clinical materials, and the published catastrophic injury verdict trackers throughout. No team member is a licensed attorney or medical provider; the team's role on this site is editorial.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cite the authority.</strong> Every rule we discuss is grounded in a specific source &mdash; the Glasgow Coma Scale literature, AANS/CNS guidelines, a state's catastrophic injury statute, an appellate opinion, peer-reviewed TBI literature, or a published life-care planning text. When we describe a rule, we name the source.</li>
    <li><strong>No legal or medical advice.</strong> Everything on this site is general and informational. TBI cases require evaluation by an experienced catastrophic injury attorney and ongoing care by neurology, rehabilitation, and neuropsychology specialists. We do not review individual cases or substitute for professional consultation.</li>
    <li><strong>No paid placements in editorial content.</strong> We do not accept money, gifts, or other consideration to recommend law firms, attorneys, rehabilitation centers, or care providers in our guides or methodology page. Separately, the case-review form on the homepage is a disclosed paid referral: submitting it shares your information with attorneys who may compensate The Click Lab Agency LLC for the introduction. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details. Revenue also comes from programmatic Google AdSense ads; we do not choose which ads run.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain language first.</strong> Neurotrauma terms (<em>GCS</em>, <em>diffuse axonal injury</em>, <em>post-concussive syndrome</em>, <em>Rancho level</em>, <em>life-care plan</em>, <em>attendant care</em>, <em>economic damages</em>, <em>non-economic damages</em>, <em>structured settlement</em>) are defined where they are first used. We assume the reader is intelligent but not formally trained.</li>
    <li><strong>Worked examples.</strong> Abstract numbers without context are hard to apply. Most guides include at least one worked example with realistic injury facts and recovery analysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Sensitive to context.</strong> TBI is often life-altering, and the reader is frequently a recently-injured patient, a spouse, or a parent in a difficult moment. We aim for content that is substantive without being clinical, informative without being alarmist.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources we rely on</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Glasgow Coma Scale</strong> &mdash; the universal acute-care severity classification, used for litigation severity grading.</li>
    <li><strong>Rancho Los Amigos Cognitive Scale</strong> &mdash; the standard cognitive recovery scale used in neuro-rehabilitation.</li>
    <li><strong>Disability Rating Scale (DRS)</strong> &mdash; widely used TBI outcome measure combining arousal, cognition, and dependency.</li>
    <li><strong>AANS / CNS clinical guidelines</strong> &mdash; American Association of Neurological Surgeons / Congress of Neurological Surgeons guidelines on severe TBI management and outcome prediction.</li>
    <li><strong>Brain Injury Association of America (BIAA)</strong> &mdash; clinical and family-facing materials on TBI prognosis and rehabilitation.</li>
    <li><strong>Life-care planning textbooks</strong> &mdash; King's <em>Life Care Planning</em>, Deutsch &amp; Sawyer's <em>Guide to Rehabilitation</em>, and the peer-reviewed life-care planning literature.</li>
    <li><strong>State catastrophic injury statutes and case law</strong> &mdash; particularly leading appellate opinions on damages caps, collateral source rules, and structured settlement requirements.</li>
    <li><strong>Verdict and settlement databases</strong> &mdash; the published catastrophic injury databases maintained by VerdictSearch, Jury Verdict Research, and specialty publications that track TBI outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Peer-reviewed TBI literature</strong> &mdash; outcome studies, prognosis literature, and the specialized literature on post-concussive syndrome, CTE, and second impact syndrome.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you find an error &mdash; an outdated severity classification, a calculator output that does not match the underlying formula, an incorrect statutory citation &mdash; please email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a> with the page URL and what is wrong. Verified corrections are typically made within a few business days. Material corrections are noted at the bottom of the affected page.</p>

  <h2>Reviewer disclosures</h2>
  <p>Our contributors are not licensed attorneys or medical providers. They are credentialed paralegals with substantive experience in catastrophic injury practice, neurotrauma medical records, and life-care planning. Reviewing legal and medical content for accuracy is part of these professionals' skill sets and is not the same as giving legal or medical advice. Nothing on this site should be construed as legal or medical advice, and nothing here creates an attorney-client or treating-provider relationship.</p>

  <h2>Conflicts of interest policy</h2>
  <p>Neither contributor personally receives referral fees from law firms, rehabilitation centers, life-care planners, or any other service provider, and neither influences or benefits from the site's separate case-review form. We do not feature or recommend specific practitioners in our guides. When a guide mentions a category of expert (e.g., "a vocational rehabilitation expert"), it does so to describe the role in litigation, not to direct readers to any specific provider. (The site itself does operate a disclosed case-review form, described in our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>, that is entirely separate from editorial content.)</p>

  <h2>Contact the editorial team</h2>
  <p>Editorial questions, citation suggestions, or interview requests: <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We respond within a few business days.</p>
</BaseLayout>
